% Oral Appliances for Obstructive Sleep Apnea: LCD L33611 and its Policy
% Article A52512 (DME MACs). A policy program of the DMEPOS model: it
% includes dmepos.le (orders, delivery, benefit category, continued need,
% refills) and states when a claim for a custom fabricated mandibular
% advancement oral appliance (E0486) is REASONABLE AND NECESSARY: the four
% criteria A-D — the in-person clinical evaluation before the sleep test, a
% Medicare-covered sleep test with qualifying values, the order written by
% the treating practitioner after reviewing the report of the sleep test,
% and the device provided and billed by a licensed dentist. A prefabricated
% oral appliance (E0485) is never covered ("will be denied as not reasonable
% and necessary. There is insufficient evidence to show that these items are
% effective therapy for OSA"): no rule concludes it. Tongue retaining
% devices and other oral appliances (A9270) are dental devices outside the
% benefit and outside this program.
%
% How the criteria are read. The sleep-test criteria are those of the PAP
% policy (pap.le), and the record facts are stated in the same words: the
% diagnosis, the in-person evaluation and its date, the sleep test (its
% date, type, AHI or RDI, events recorded, whether the DME supplier ran it),
% the symptoms documented (the shared template "the medical record of B
% documents C"). The AHI template stands for the AHI of a facility study and
% the RDI of a home sleep test alike, as the LCD treats them. Criterion B has
% three alternatives: (1) an AHI of 15 or more with 30 events, (2) an AHI of
% 5 to 14 with 10 events and a documented symptom, (3) an AHI over 30 and a
% PAP device the beneficiary cannot tolerate or the treating practitioner
% finds contraindicated. The last is one finding, the judged template of
% dmepos.le "the finding F about B is established" (or unsupported), with
% who found it and why:
%   pap device unsuitable (criterion B3: the beneficiary is not able to
%   tolerate a PAP device, or the treating practitioner determines that the
%   use of a PAP device is contraindicated).
% Criteria C and D are what the record states: that the order was written by
% the treating practitioner following a review of the report of the sleep
% test, and that the device was provided and billed by a licensed dentist.
% That the sleep test was ordered by the treating practitioner, FDA-approved
% and run by a qualified Medicare provider (the LCD's "Sleep Tests"
% paragraph) is not modelled, as in pap.le; a test the DME supplier ran is
% excluded expressly (Policy Article A52512).
%
% The Policy Article's policy-specific requirements are the narrative note
% of the in-person evaluation (the evaluation is criterion A) and the
% KX/GA/GZ modifiers (billing): the policy declares no documentation
% requirement of its own. Not modelled: the follow-up care of the first 90
% days included in the payment, the 5-year reasonable useful lifetime and
% replacement, repairs, the PDAC coding verification of E0486.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    % the record (the sleep-test facts in the words of pap.le)
    *a beneficiary* has a diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea; undefined.
    *a beneficiary* had an in-person clinical evaluation by the treating practitioner on *a date*; undefined.
    the sleep test of *a beneficiary* was performed on *a date*; undefined.
    the type of the sleep test of *a beneficiary* is *a type*; undefined.
    the AHI of the sleep test of *a beneficiary* is *a number*; undefined.
    the sleep test of *a beneficiary* recorded *a number* events; undefined.
    the sleep test of *a beneficiary* was performed by the DME supplier; undefined.
    the order for *an item* was written by the treating practitioner following a review of the report of the sleep test; undefined.
    *an item* is provided and billed by a licensed dentist; undefined.
    % derived
    *an item* is a custom fabricated mandibular advancement oral appliance.
    *a beneficiary* was clinically evaluated before the sleep test for an oral appliance.
    the sleep test of *a beneficiary* is a Medicare covered sleep test for an oral appliance.
    the sleep test of *a beneficiary* meets the oral appliance AHI criterion.
    *a condition* is a qualifying symptom for an oral appliance.

the knowledge base oral_appliances includes these resources:
    dmepos.

the knowledge base oral_appliances includes:

LCD L33611 is published at "https://www.cms.gov/medicare-coverage-database/view/lcd.aspx?lcdid=33611".
the text of LCD L33611 is at "sources/lcd/L33611.txt".
Policy Article A52512 is published at "https://www.cms.gov/medicare-coverage-database/view/article.aspx?articleid=52512".
the text of Policy Article A52512 is at "sources/article/A52512.txt".

% What a custom fabricated mandibular advancement oral appliance is (the
% Policy Article's coding guidelines).
rule oral_custom_fabricated with provenance Policy Article A52512,
        confer "HCPCS code E0486 may only be used for custom fabricated mandibular advancement devices":
an item is a custom fabricated mandibular advancement oral appliance
    if the HCPCS code of the item is "E0486".

% The symptoms of criterion B2.
excessive daytime sleepiness is a qualifying symptom for an oral appliance,
    as stated in LCD L33611, confer "Excessive daytime sleepiness, impaired cognition, mood disorders, or insomnia".
impaired cognition is a qualifying symptom for an oral appliance,
    as stated in LCD L33611, confer "Excessive daytime sleepiness, impaired cognition, mood disorders, or insomnia".
mood disorder is a qualifying symptom for an oral appliance,
    as stated in LCD L33611, confer "Excessive daytime sleepiness, impaired cognition, mood disorders, or insomnia".
insomnia is a qualifying symptom for an oral appliance,
    as stated in LCD L33611, confer "Excessive daytime sleepiness, impaired cognition, mood disorders, or insomnia".
hypertension is a qualifying symptom for an oral appliance,
    as stated in LCD L33611, confer "Hypertension, ischemic heart disease, or history of stroke".
ischemic heart disease is a qualifying symptom for an oral appliance,
    as stated in LCD L33611, confer "Hypertension, ischemic heart disease, or history of stroke".
history of stroke is a qualifying symptom for an oral appliance,
    as stated in LCD L33611, confer "Hypertension, ischemic heart disease, or history of stroke".

section applicability is:

% The Policy Article's policy-specific requirements are the narrative note
% of the evaluation (criterion A) and the claim modifiers (billing): none of
% the policy's own.
oral_appliances has no specific documentation requirements.

% The codes this policy decides, and their benefit: durable medical
% equipment (Policy Article A52512).
rule oral_codes with provenance Policy Article A52512,
        confer "Oral appliances used to treat obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) are covered under the Durable Medical Equipment benefit":
the policy for code a code is oral_appliances
    if the code is in ["E0485", "E0486"].

rule oral_benefit with provenance Policy Article A52512,
        confer "Oral appliances used to treat obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) are covered under the Durable Medical Equipment benefit":
the benefit category of code a code is durable medical equipment
    if the policy for code the code is oral_appliances.

section question is:

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The custom fabricated appliance: criteria A-D. A prefabricated appliance
% (E0485) has no rule: "A prefabricated oral appliance (E0485) will be
% denied as not reasonable and necessary."
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule oral_covered with provenance LCD L33611,
        confer "A custom fabricated mandibular advancement oral appliance (E0486) used to treat obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is covered if criteria A - D are met":
a claim is reasonable and necessary
    if the claim is for an item
    and the item is a custom fabricated mandibular advancement oral appliance
    and the item is furnished to a beneficiary
    and the beneficiary has a diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea
    and the beneficiary was clinically evaluated before the sleep test for an oral appliance
    and the sleep test of the beneficiary is a Medicare covered sleep test for an oral appliance
    and the sleep test of the beneficiary meets the oral appliance AHI criterion
    and the order for the item was written by the treating practitioner following a review of the report of the sleep test
    and the item is provided and billed by a licensed dentist.

% Criterion A.
rule oral_criterion_a with provenance LCD L33611,
        confer "The beneficiary has an in-person clinical evaluation by the treating practitioner prior to the sleep test to assess the beneficiary for obstructive sleep apnea testing":
a beneficiary was clinically evaluated before the sleep test for an oral appliance
    if the beneficiary had an in-person clinical evaluation by the treating practitioner on a date
    and the sleep test of the beneficiary was performed on an other date
    and the date is before or equal to the other date.

% Criterion B: a Medicare-covered sleep test — a facility polysomnogram or an
% inpatient or home sleep test — that the DME supplier had no part in
% (Policy Article A52512).
rule oral_sleep_test with provenance LCD L33611,
        confer "The sleep test must be either a polysomnogram performed in a facility-based laboratory (Type I study) or an inpatient hospital-based or home-based sleep test (HST) (Types II, III, IV, Other)":
the sleep test of a beneficiary is a Medicare covered sleep test for an oral appliance
    if the type of the sleep test of the beneficiary is a type
    and the type is in [facility polysomnogram, inpatient sleep test, home sleep test]
    and it is not the case that
        the sleep test of the beneficiary was performed by the DME supplier.

% Criterion B, alternative 1.
rule oral_criterion_b1 with provenance LCD L33611,
        confer "The apnea-hypopnea index (AHI) or Respiratory Disturbance Index (RDI) is greater than or equal to 15 events per hour with a minimum of 30 events":
the sleep test of a beneficiary meets the oral appliance AHI criterion
    if the AHI of the sleep test of the beneficiary is a number
    and the number >= 15
    and the sleep test of the beneficiary recorded an other number events
    and the other number >= 30.

% Criterion B, alternative 2.
rule oral_criterion_b2 with provenance LCD L33611,
        confer "The AHI or RDI is greater than or equal to 5 and less than or equal to 14 events per hour with a minimum of 10 events and documentation of":
the sleep test of a beneficiary meets the oral appliance AHI criterion
    if the AHI of the sleep test of the beneficiary is a number
    and the number >= 5
    and the number <= 14
    and the sleep test of the beneficiary recorded an other number events
    and the other number >= 10
    and the medical record of the beneficiary documents a condition
    and the condition is a qualifying symptom for an oral appliance.

% Criterion B, alternative 3: an AHI or RDI over 30 and a PAP device the
% beneficiary is not able to tolerate or the treating practitioner finds
% contraindicated (the finding's reasons; "The treating practitioner
% determines that the use of a PAP device is contraindicated").
rule oral_criterion_b3 with provenance LCD L33611,
        confer "The beneficiary is not able to tolerate a positive airway pressure (PAP) device":
the sleep test of a beneficiary meets the oral appliance AHI criterion
    if the AHI of the sleep test of the beneficiary is a number
    and the number > 30
    and the finding pap device unsuitable about the beneficiary is established.

% Criteria C and D are the record facts of the covering rule: "The device is
% ordered by the treating practitioner following a review of the report of
% the sleep test" and "The device is provided and billed for by a licensed
% dentist (DDS or DMD)".
